From 1f200714c1490ffdc1d4dff7e508e5e6f6956457 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Syncony <110027245+Syncony@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:14:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] feat(anytls): heartbeat health checks and latency-aware
 session pick

Client:
- Ping idle sessions with cmdHeartRequest at a random interval of
  10-30s, so heartbeats neither keep a mobile radio awake nor form a
  regular traffic pattern. Pongs give an RTT estimate (RFC 6298
  SRTT/RTTVAR).
- Close idle sessions whose ping is overdue (3-10s, from the RTT), so a
  request never lands on a session that died in the pool.
- Pick the idle session expected to answer soonest instead of the newest.

Negotiation:
- The client adds "x-hk=1" to its settings; a patched server echoes it in
  cmdServerSettings. Unpatched peers ignore unknown keys.
- With a patched server, SYNACK (sent before dialing the destination) is
  also an RTT sample and the reused-session SYNACK deadline follows the
  RTT, never below the original 3s.
- A late SYNACK no longer closes a session that received frames after the
  SYN, so one slow stream does not tear down the busy ones beside it.

Server:
- Dynamic record sizing: after 1s idle, data frames start at one TCP
  segment per TLS record and grow by a segment per frame up to 16 KiB.
  Keeping the congestion window open across idle periods is left to the
  kernel (net.ipv4.tcp_slow_start_after_idle=0) instead of server pings.
